1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,450
Day ten. The ground shakes.

2
00:00:02,470 --> 00:00:05,286
Dirt pours from the ceiling of your
tunnel.

3
00:00:05,698 --> 00:00:08,189
Outside, the sky is on fire.

4
00:00:08,298 --> 00:00:13,703
And at the door, something too big to fit
inside is breathing.

5
00:00:15,403 --> 00:00:18,950
Ten days ago, you were asleep in your own
bed.

6
00:00:19,251 --> 00:00:23,100
You have 10 days before the asteroid that
killed the dinosaurs.

7
00:00:23,512 --> 00:00:26,081
Every big animal you see will die.

8
00:00:26,101 --> 00:00:27,117
You won't.

9
00:00:27,645 --> 00:00:29,068
If you stay small.

10
00:00:32,317 --> 00:00:34,761
The night before was like any other.

11
00:00:34,987 --> 00:00:40,276
Homework, a documentary about the
dinosaurs, your phone at 23%.

12
00:00:40,432 --> 00:00:42,215
"I'll charge it in the morning."

13
00:00:42,325 --> 00:00:47,645
In your backpack, ready for school: two
textbooks, a sandwich, two chocolate

14
00:00:47,665 --> 00:00:49,935
bars, half a bottle of water.

15
00:00:50,266 --> 00:00:53,383
And a library book you wanted to read in
the break tomorrow.

16
00:00:53,551 --> 00:00:56,390
The Last Day of the Dinosaurs.

17
00:00:56,691 --> 00:00:58,900
In the morning, there's no ceiling.

18
00:00:58,920 --> 00:01:02,096
There are leaves, each one bigger than
your bed.

19
00:01:02,264 --> 00:01:04,740
The air is hot and wet. No cars.

20
00:01:04,760 --> 00:01:05,962
No voices.

21
00:01:06,374 --> 00:01:09,852
Next to you, something rises from the
ground like a tree trunk.

22
00:01:10,639 --> 00:01:12,004
It isn't a tree.

23
00:01:12,536 --> 00:01:18,495
It has claws. The foot lifts, slams down
one step ahead, and the ground

24
00:01:18,515 --> 00:01:24,001
jumps under you. You don't breathe until
the footsteps fade.

25
00:01:24,301 --> 00:01:26,641
Your backpack is lying next to you.

26
00:01:26,704 --> 00:01:28,870
Your phone is still in your pocket.

27
00:01:29,282 --> 00:01:34,443
No signal. No internet. Just one message
that wasn't there last night.

28
00:01:34,854 --> 00:01:38,768
"Impact: 10 days. 3,000 kilometers south."

29
00:01:38,788 --> 00:01:41,778
A prank. A dream. It has to be.

30
00:01:42,108 --> 00:01:45,876
You switch it off at 22%, and you start
walking.

31
00:01:46,175 --> 00:01:50,683
All afternoon, you look for a road, a
wire, a plane in the sky.

32
00:01:50,703 --> 00:01:52,785
Anything. There's nothing.

33
00:01:53,441 --> 00:01:55,998
In the evening, you find the owner of the
foot.

34
00:01:56,018 --> 00:02:02,618
A T. rex. 12 meters, nose to tail, with a
long pale scar across its snout.

35
00:02:03,030 --> 00:02:05,878
For one second, its head turns toward you.

36
00:02:05,898 --> 00:02:07,761
Then it walks on.

37
00:02:08,967 --> 00:02:13,677
It's not a dream. That night you squeeze
in between the roots of a giant

38
00:02:13,697 --> 00:02:16,989
tree. You're shaking. You don't want to
sleep.

39
00:02:17,320 --> 00:02:22,283
In the dark, you open your backpack,
looking for anything that makes sense.

40
00:02:22,521 --> 00:02:25,787
The library book. On the cover, a T.

41
00:02:25,807 --> 00:02:29,667
rex skeleton in a museum. You don't look
closely.

42
00:02:30,079 --> 00:02:33,357
Inside, chapter seven. Who Survived.

43
00:02:33,687 --> 00:02:37,501
Animals that were small. Animals that hid
underground.

44
00:02:37,739 --> 00:02:40,241
Animals that lived near fresh water.

45
00:02:40,572 --> 00:02:44,095
On the back page, with your school pen,
you write a plan.

46
00:02:46,707 --> 00:02:50,008
Three things. Ten days. None of them done.

47
00:02:50,176 --> 00:02:52,072
You don't decide to fall asleep.

48
00:02:52,092 --> 00:02:54,792
Your body decides for you.

49
00:02:55,842 --> 00:02:58,771
In the morning, there's a small hole by
the roots.

50
00:02:58,791 --> 00:03:00,852
A furry face pokes out.

51
00:03:01,264 --> 00:03:04,347
Mouse-sized. Sniffs. Disappears.

52
00:03:04,758 --> 00:03:10,330
You name him Pip. The T. rex needs
mountains of meat every day.

53
00:03:10,568 --> 00:03:13,407
Pip needs a few seeds and a hole to hide
in.

54
00:03:13,819 --> 00:03:15,566
And your book agrees.

55
00:03:15,978 --> 00:03:20,312
Scientists think that after the impact,
almost no land animal heavier than

56
00:03:20,332 --> 00:03:23,298
about 25 kilograms survived.

57
00:03:24,086 --> 00:03:27,912
You weigh 70. So you'll have to think like
Pip.

58
00:03:28,242 --> 00:03:33,103
Rule one: stay small. You eat half the
sandwich.

59
00:03:33,123 --> 00:03:37,054
You save the rest. Then you watch what Pip
eats.

60
00:03:37,384 --> 00:03:40,769
Small red berries, seeds from one kind of
fern.

61
00:03:40,937 --> 00:03:44,716
That's no guarantee. Plenty of things a
mouse can eat would kill you.

62
00:03:45,047 --> 00:03:48,699
But he's a mammal, like you, and the
sandwich won't last.

63
00:03:49,029 --> 00:03:51,113
You eat one berry. You wait.

64
00:03:51,281 --> 00:03:53,459
By evening, nothing has happened.

65
00:03:53,696 --> 00:03:55,699
You fill your pockets.

66
00:03:56,299 --> 00:03:58,717
Day three. The bottle is empty.

67
00:03:58,737 --> 00:04:00,299
Your throat is burning.

68
00:04:00,711 --> 00:04:04,571
You follow the sound of water to a wide,
muddy river.

69
00:04:04,809 --> 00:04:07,114
At the edge, a log is floating.

70
00:04:07,995 --> 00:04:09,080
It blinks.

71
00:04:09,868 --> 00:04:13,067
It isn't a log. It's a crocodile.

72
00:04:13,367 --> 00:04:15,358
So you don't drink from the river.

73
00:04:15,689 --> 00:04:20,409
A few steps back from the bank, in the
sand, you dig a pit and wait.

74
00:04:20,577 --> 00:04:22,835
Water seeps in through the sand.

75
00:04:23,166 --> 00:04:28,301
Slow. Muddy. But cleaner than the river,
and far from the teeth.

76
00:04:28,539 --> 00:04:33,421
You fill the bottle. In the shallow pools,
small fish are trapped.

77
00:04:33,659 --> 00:04:36,521
You catch three and dry them on a hot
rock.

78
00:04:36,852 --> 00:04:41,838
No fire needed. It's warm here, almost
tropical, even at night.

79
00:04:42,366 --> 00:04:45,357
From now on, every morning starts at the
pools.

80
00:04:47,469 --> 00:04:51,664
The book says freshwater animals, turtles,
frogs, even crocodiles, made

81
00:04:51,684 --> 00:04:53,907
it through far better than most.

82
00:04:54,238 --> 00:04:57,460
Rule two: stay near water.

83
00:04:58,510 --> 00:04:59,666
Day four.

84
00:05:00,077 --> 00:05:02,971
The ground shakes again. Not one footstep.

85
00:05:02,991 --> 00:05:07,839
Hundreds. A herd of duck-billed dinosaurs
comes along the river.

86
00:05:08,355 --> 00:05:11,276
Then a deep sound rolls in from far away.

87
00:05:11,513 --> 00:05:14,433
You feel it in your chest more than your
ears.

88
00:05:14,764 --> 00:05:17,603
The herd panics. Trees snap.

89
00:05:17,713 --> 00:05:20,320
You throw yourself behind a fallen log.

90
00:05:20,732 --> 00:05:23,245
When the dust settles, one of them didn't
get up.

91
00:05:23,413 --> 00:05:24,903
And it has something you need.

92
00:05:24,923 --> 00:05:27,109
A shoulder blade. Flat. Wide.

93
00:05:27,129 --> 00:05:28,609
The size of a paddle.

94
00:05:29,137 --> 00:05:32,197
From today, your shovel is a dinosaur.

95
00:05:32,796 --> 00:05:33,963
Day five.

96
00:05:34,375 --> 00:05:36,448
You read the page you've been avoiding.

97
00:05:36,616 --> 00:05:41,161
Within an hour of the impact, burning
debris falls back through the sky,

98
00:05:41,225 --> 00:05:46,908
all over the world. Scientists still argue
about how hot it got.

99
00:05:47,076 --> 00:05:50,612
In many places, hot enough to set forests
on fire.

100
00:05:50,942 --> 00:05:53,363
On the surface, there's nowhere to hide.

101
00:05:53,891 --> 00:05:55,941
Under a meter of soil, maybe.

102
00:05:56,469 --> 00:05:59,935
Rule three: stay underground.

103
00:06:00,235 --> 00:06:04,107
You find a clay bank above the river and
start digging.

104
00:06:04,407 --> 00:06:05,864
Now the math.

105
00:06:06,276 --> 00:06:12,084
With a bone, in clay, a cave big enough to
crouch in grows about 30 centimeters

106
00:06:12,104 --> 00:06:16,636
a day. To get a meter of soil over your
head, you have to go two and a half

107
00:06:16,656 --> 00:06:19,878
meters in. That's eight days of work.

108
00:06:20,290 --> 00:06:22,467
The asteroid hits on day ten.

109
00:06:22,798 --> 00:06:28,505
You have five. You dig until it's too dark
to see, then sleep in the mud

110
00:06:28,525 --> 00:06:30,702
by the hole.

111
00:06:31,303 --> 00:06:34,107
Day six. Your hands are blistered.

112
00:06:34,275 --> 00:06:36,708
You're in as deep as your arms can reach.

113
00:06:37,495 --> 00:06:39,951
Then the roof comes down on you.

114
00:06:40,119 --> 00:06:42,331
You crawl out coughing dirt.

115
00:06:43,212 --> 00:06:46,643
Everything you dug, gone. Four days left.

116
00:06:46,881 --> 00:06:50,209
Eight days of digging. You sit in the mud.

117
00:06:50,229 --> 00:06:54,751
And there, in the bank next to your ruin,
a tiny new hole.

118
00:06:55,690 --> 00:06:57,620
Pip followed you to the river.

119
00:06:57,640 --> 00:07:01,234
His hole is tiny. And it doesn't collapse.

120
00:07:01,553 --> 00:07:03,591
Because Pip doesn't dig a cave.

121
00:07:04,212 --> 00:07:09,535
Pip digs a tunnel exactly as wide as Pip,
and one small nest at the end.

122
00:07:09,703 --> 00:07:11,939
So you stop digging a cave.

123
00:07:12,350 --> 00:07:15,421
You dig a tunnel exactly as wide as your
shoulders.

124
00:07:15,589 --> 00:07:18,963
A quarter of the dirt. Four times as fast.

125
00:07:19,200 --> 00:07:23,464
And you shape the roof into an arch,
because an arch pushes the weight into

126
00:07:23,484 --> 00:07:26,138
the walls instead of down on you.

127
00:07:26,793 --> 00:07:31,710
The entrance faces north, away from where
the asteroid will hit.

128
00:07:32,366 --> 00:07:33,820
Maybe it makes no difference.

129
00:07:33,840 --> 00:07:36,059
It feels right. Stay small.

130
00:07:36,079 --> 00:07:38,315
Even when you dig.

131
00:07:40,814 --> 00:07:43,781
By dark, the tunnel swallows you to the
waist.

132
00:07:44,193 --> 00:07:47,934
That night you sleep with your head
underground and your boots outside.

133
00:07:47,954 --> 00:07:49,655
It's a start.

134
00:07:50,706 --> 00:07:51,838
Day seven.

135
00:07:52,494 --> 00:07:56,529
Pip doesn't run anymore. He's waiting at
the entrance in the morning.

136
00:07:56,697 --> 00:07:59,989
You split your last chocolate with him,
and you dig.

137
00:08:02,357 --> 00:08:04,917
By noon, the blisters have burst.

138
00:08:05,085 --> 00:08:08,202
Your palms are raw and bleeding into the
clay.

139
00:08:08,370 --> 00:08:11,616
And you know a hand that breaks today digs
nothing tomorrow.

140
00:08:11,783 --> 00:08:14,890
So you stop. Even with three days left.

141
00:08:15,301 --> 00:08:17,301
Even if the end is coming anyway.

142
00:08:17,321 --> 00:08:20,184
For one afternoon, you rest.

143
00:08:20,484 --> 00:08:22,937
You switch the phone on. Just for a
minute.

144
00:08:22,957 --> 00:08:24,609
The minute becomes an hour.

145
00:08:24,629 --> 00:08:27,881
Photos. Your room. Your mom in the
kitchen.

146
00:08:27,901 --> 00:08:30,101
Your friends outside school.

147
00:08:30,432 --> 00:08:32,583
Then a video, from a few months ago.

148
00:08:32,603 --> 00:08:38,086
A class trip to a museum. You, laughing at
the camera, a giant skeleton

149
00:08:38,106 --> 00:08:42,208
behind you. "If they ever sent me back to
the dinosaurs?

150
00:08:42,228 --> 00:08:44,461
I'd survive. Easy."

151
00:08:44,989 --> 00:08:48,280
Your friends laughing behind the phone.

152
00:08:49,980 --> 00:08:52,506
Why are you here? What happened?

153
00:08:52,569 --> 00:08:54,247
Maybe you're still asleep.

154
00:08:54,640 --> 00:08:56,655
Maybe this is all a dream.

155
00:08:56,892 --> 00:08:58,895
You look down at your hands.

156
00:08:59,776 --> 00:09:03,118
The pain is real. It's not a dream.

157
00:09:04,818 --> 00:09:07,018
You always said you'd survive.

158
00:09:07,899 --> 00:09:09,345
Now you have to prove it.

159
00:09:09,873 --> 00:09:12,186
When you switch it off, it's at 11%.

160
00:09:12,206 --> 00:09:14,337
Ten percent, gone on home.

161
00:09:14,753 --> 00:09:19,814
It was worth it. That night, you fit all
the way inside.

162
00:09:20,214 --> 00:09:23,668
You wake up, and Pip is asleep in your
jacket pocket.

163
00:09:24,312 --> 00:09:26,116
Warm. Breathing.

164
00:09:26,504 --> 00:09:29,355
Seven days in a world with no one.

165
00:09:30,410 --> 00:09:33,167
And now you have someone.

166
00:09:34,218 --> 00:09:37,243
Day eight. You dig until your arms shake.

167
00:09:37,480 --> 00:09:42,441
Two and a half meters. And at the very
end, a nest, just big enough to sit

168
00:09:42,461 --> 00:09:43,361
up in.

169
00:09:45,671 --> 00:09:47,814
A meter of soil over your head.

170
00:09:48,225 --> 00:09:53,618
That night, for the first time in eight
days, nothing can step on you.

171
00:09:55,318 --> 00:09:58,723
At midnight, you wake. Scratching at the
entrance.

172
00:09:58,743 --> 00:10:02,093
Then sniffing. Then something coming in.

173
00:10:02,505 --> 00:10:04,725
In the dark, two yellow eyes.

174
00:10:04,745 --> 00:10:08,629
A feathered hunter. Fast. Only as tall as
your knee.

175
00:10:08,867 --> 00:10:11,973
Not a T. rex. Small enough to fit in your
tunnel.

176
00:10:12,385 --> 00:10:16,294
Small doesn't mean safe. Your headlamp
lights it up.

177
00:10:16,314 --> 00:10:17,632
It doesn't care.

178
00:10:18,048 --> 00:10:21,979
It lunges. Claws rip across your right
forearm.

179
00:10:22,309 --> 00:10:25,752
No knife. No fire. No room to swing a
bone.

180
00:10:26,539 --> 00:10:29,135
Just one thing this world has never seen.

181
00:10:29,465 --> 00:10:31,674
You switch the phone on. 11%.

182
00:10:31,694 --> 00:10:35,415
You hit the alarm and point the flashlight
straight into its face.

183
00:10:35,827 --> 00:10:40,280
A sound sixty-six million years too early
fills the tunnel.

184
00:10:40,998 --> 00:10:43,140
It shrieks, and it's gone.

185
00:10:43,440 --> 00:10:45,339
But it could come back.

186
00:10:45,994 --> 00:10:47,962
Blood drips from your arm.

187
00:10:48,130 --> 00:10:51,341
Pip creeps out of your pocket and sits by
your hand.

188
00:10:51,579 --> 00:10:53,126
You can't leave the light on.

189
00:10:53,146 --> 00:10:57,015
It would be dead in an hour, and dawn is
five hours away.

190
00:10:57,035 --> 00:10:58,481
So you count.

191
00:10:59,009 --> 00:11:02,312
Ten seconds of light. One minute of dark.

192
00:11:02,724 --> 00:11:07,142
Every time the dark comes back, you listen
for claws.

193
00:11:08,842 --> 00:11:11,507
When the sky turns grey, you switch it
off.

194
00:11:12,585 --> 00:11:13,729
3%.

195
00:11:14,329 --> 00:11:16,352
Day nine. You have a fever.

196
00:11:16,372 --> 00:11:20,814
The wound is hot. You wrap it in a strip
torn from your t-shirt.

197
00:11:21,144 --> 00:11:23,252
The entrance let a hunter in.

198
00:11:23,420 --> 00:11:26,375
Tomorrow, it has to keep the whole world
out.

199
00:11:26,786 --> 00:11:30,470
One-handed, you drag stones up from the
river and stack them around the

200
00:11:30,490 --> 00:11:33,770
opening. Last, a big flat stone.

201
00:11:34,298 --> 00:11:38,388
From inside, you can drag it across like a
door, and push it aside to get

202
00:11:38,408 --> 00:11:39,308
out.

203
00:11:39,708 --> 00:11:43,000
When it's closed, it leaves a gap a hand
wide.

204
00:11:43,412 --> 00:11:45,995
Enough for air. Not enough for teeth.

205
00:11:46,407 --> 00:11:49,734
The sun is going down when the door slides
into place.

206
00:11:52,171 --> 00:11:54,708
Just in time. Two out of three.

207
00:11:54,946 --> 00:11:57,411
Twelve dried fish won't last a winter.

208
00:11:57,431 --> 00:11:59,921
And you're out of days.

209
00:12:00,972 --> 00:12:03,660
You switch the phone on one more time.

210
00:12:03,724 --> 00:12:05,448
The message has changed.

211
00:12:05,860 --> 00:12:09,906
"Impact: tomorrow." You switch it off.

212
00:12:10,206 --> 00:12:13,300
That night you slide the door aside and
climb out.

213
00:12:13,410 --> 00:12:18,406
The sky is full of stars. From across the
river comes that low, familiar

214
00:12:18,426 --> 00:12:23,784
call. The same T. rex. The last night of
the dinosaurs.

215
00:12:23,883 --> 00:12:28,630
And only you know. It isn't a strange
morning.

216
00:12:28,961 --> 00:12:30,882
That's what makes it strange.

217
00:12:30,992 --> 00:12:34,003
Birds are singing. Insects are buzzing.

218
00:12:34,333 --> 00:12:37,544
A few triceratops are drinking across the
river.

219
00:12:38,072 --> 00:12:43,121
None of them know. Then, in the south, a
light.

220
00:12:43,533 --> 00:12:44,781
Not like the sun.

221
00:12:45,662 --> 00:12:49,383
Like someone switched the sky on by
mistake.

222
00:12:49,683 --> 00:12:52,789
Your book said the shaking comes within
minutes.

223
00:12:53,670 --> 00:12:54,639
It's right.

224
00:12:55,295 --> 00:12:57,205
The ground starts to move.

225
00:12:57,373 --> 00:13:00,151
Not like a footstep. Like the whole world.

226
00:13:00,171 --> 00:13:04,648
The river pulls back. Then it comes back,
the wrong way.

227
00:13:04,816 --> 00:13:08,769
A wall of water runs upstream and throws
fish onto the bank.

228
00:13:09,007 --> 00:13:13,030
It climbs up the bank, and stops a hand
below your door.

229
00:13:13,268 --> 00:13:17,581
When it pulls back, you run down and grab
every fish you can carry.

230
00:13:18,369 --> 00:13:19,710
Food for the dark.

231
00:13:21,730 --> 00:13:23,825
The last thing on your list.

232
00:13:24,237 --> 00:13:26,844
The end of the world just handed it to
you.

233
00:13:27,082 --> 00:13:32,936
This isn't imagination. In North Dakota,
scientists found fossil fish with

234
00:13:32,956 --> 00:13:37,676
tiny glass beads from this impact stuck in
their gills.

235
00:13:37,976 --> 00:13:41,686
Then those same glass beads start falling
from the sky.

236
00:13:42,341 --> 00:13:45,363
Small and hot, like burning hail.

237
00:13:45,383 --> 00:13:50,773
The sky turns red. You drag the door shut,
hold Pip tight and crawl to the

238
00:13:50,793 --> 00:13:55,641
end of the tunnel. Outside, the first
trees catch fire.

239
00:13:55,972 --> 00:13:59,124
By evening, the whole forest is burning.

240
00:13:59,424 --> 00:14:02,147
And then, outside, footsteps.

241
00:14:02,454 --> 00:14:05,023
Heavy. Slow. Close.

242
00:14:05,528 --> 00:14:09,865
Something huge collapses at your door, and
its shadow covers the gap.

243
00:14:10,172 --> 00:14:14,298
The T. rex. From day one. The scar across
its snout.

244
00:14:14,687 --> 00:14:17,325
Its huge snout is right against the
stones.

245
00:14:17,345 --> 00:14:19,743
Its hot breath fills your tunnel.

246
00:14:20,403 --> 00:14:22,937
It can't get in. It doesn't even try.

247
00:14:22,957 --> 00:14:27,851
It only came for the cool soil at your
door, away from the fire.

248
00:14:28,151 --> 00:14:32,441
All night, from the other side of the
stones, you hear it breathing.

249
00:14:32,853 --> 00:14:35,553
Each breath slower.

250
00:14:37,253 --> 00:14:39,128
Until morning.

251
00:14:40,828 --> 00:14:42,285
Then it stops.

252
00:14:43,166 --> 00:14:47,015
The last dinosaur you saw died at your
door.

253
00:14:48,065 --> 00:14:52,050
In the days after, the river rises again
and buries the T.

254
00:14:52,070 --> 00:14:55,885
rex in thick mud. Buried fast in mud.

255
00:14:56,122 --> 00:14:58,346
That's how bones become fossils.

256
00:14:58,514 --> 00:15:00,247
You open your book to the cover.

257
00:15:00,267 --> 00:15:04,023
The museum skeleton. And this time, you
look closely.

258
00:15:04,985 --> 00:15:09,531
A long scar, healed into the bone of the
snout.

259
00:15:11,231 --> 00:15:14,601
The same skeleton that stood behind you in
that video.

260
00:15:14,621 --> 00:15:16,531
The one you joked in front of.

261
00:15:16,862 --> 00:15:20,757
Sixty-six million years from now, it will
stand in a museum.

262
00:15:20,995 --> 00:15:25,445
And one day, you'll stand in front of it,
laughing, with no idea you were

263
00:15:25,465 --> 00:15:29,689
here when it died. Then the dark comes.

264
00:15:29,927 --> 00:15:34,772
Soot from fires all over the world, and
dust from the impact, cover the

265
00:15:34,792 --> 00:15:37,930
whole sky. Sunlight can't get through.

266
00:15:37,950 --> 00:15:41,241
Plants can't grow. And it gets cold.

267
00:15:41,897 --> 00:15:45,154
Scientists think this impact winter lasted
months.

268
00:15:45,217 --> 00:15:49,728
Maybe years. The world gave you one gift
on its last day.

269
00:15:50,058 --> 00:15:53,652
Fire. You carried an ember in from the
burning forest.

270
00:15:53,890 --> 00:15:58,041
You keep a small fire right at the gap,
never deep inside.

271
00:15:58,267 --> 00:16:01,965
In a closed tunnel, smoke kills quietly.

272
00:16:02,284 --> 00:16:05,262
And at night, Pip sleeps against your
neck.

273
00:16:05,500 --> 00:16:08,873
Two small bodies, keeping each other warm.

274
00:16:09,173 --> 00:16:11,127
The phone won't turn on anymore.

275
00:16:11,147 --> 00:16:15,600
The pen is dry. So you scratch lines on
the tunnel wall with a sharp stone.

276
00:16:15,721 --> 00:16:18,874
Inventory. Days after the end: 40.

277
00:16:19,158 --> 00:16:21,966
Still dark. Dried fish: 7.

278
00:16:21,986 --> 00:16:22,979
Running low.

279
00:16:23,802 --> 00:16:27,376
Dinosaurs left: zero. Friends: one.

280
00:16:27,396 --> 00:16:31,725
Mouse-sized. You repeat the rules to
yourself.

281
00:16:32,137 --> 00:16:34,596
Stay small. Stay near water.

282
00:16:34,616 --> 00:16:36,039
Stay underground.

283
00:16:36,566 --> 00:16:39,556
And a new one, that no book ever taught
you.

284
00:16:40,212 --> 00:16:43,852
Stay small… but don't stay alone.

285
00:16:44,152 --> 00:16:47,548
Small animals like Pip made it out of this
darkness.

286
00:16:47,786 --> 00:16:53,617
Millions of years later, from small
mammals like him, came horses, whales,

287
00:16:53,637 --> 00:17:00,339
apes. And people. Sixty-six million years
later, one of Pip's great-great-great-
grandchildren

288
00:17:00,359 --> 00:17:05,897
is watching this video. And just then,
from outside the tunnel, a sound.

289
00:17:06,777 --> 00:17:11,404
A bird, singing. You cross out a line on
the wall, and write it again.

290
00:17:12,482 --> 00:17:16,888
Dinosaurs left: zero… except the birds.

291
00:17:17,189 --> 00:17:20,458
On day one, what would you have done
first?

292
00:17:20,985 --> 00:17:25,006
Tell me in the comments. And if you want
to know what you and Pip did after

293
00:17:25,026 --> 00:17:27,888
the dark, tell me that too.

294
00:17:28,188 --> 00:17:30,388
Stay small. Stay alive.
